## Adsorptive removal of harmful gases
The possible use of MOFs for the removal of gaseous sulfur compounds has been recently investigated. The presence of open metal sites and H-bonding donor–acceptor groups in these adsorbents have been proposed as suitable features for the efficient removal of S-compounds. In this context, two different classes of MIL systems (MIL stands for Materials of the Institute Lavoisier), the series MIL-53(Al, Cr) and MIL-47(V) $[\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})(\mathbf{bdc})]$ ${\mathrm{bdc}}=1{,}4$ -terephthalate; $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{O}$ for $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{V}^{4+}$ , OH for $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ ) and the series MIL-100(Cr) $[\mathbf{Cr}_{3}\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tc})_{2}]$ (btc $^{=1,3,5}$ -tricarboxylate) and MIL-101(Cr) $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{F}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\right]$ have been explored for the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .4,5 Small-pore MIL-47(V) and $\mathbf{MIL-}53(\mathrm{Al},\ \mathrm{Cr})$ exhibit reversible adsorption behaviour under $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ pressure. Moreover, IR measurements and molecular simulations reveal that the MIL-47(V) framework remains rigid upon $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ adsorption up to a pressure of $1.8\mathrm{MPa}$ while MIL-53(Cr) shows a flexible behaviour undertaking two structural transitions: the first one from a large pore form (LP) to its narrow pore version (NP) at very low pressure and the second one from the NP to the LP form at higher pressure. These structural transitions explain the two-step shape of the adsorption isotherm of MIL-53(Cr). Moreover, the arrangements of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules within the pores of these MIL species have been investigated. At an initial stage, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ molecules form hydrogen bonds, either as a hydrogen donor (in MIL-47(V)) or hydrogen acceptor (in MIL-53(Cr)) with the $\upmu_{2}{\bf-O}$ and $\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ groups, respectively, at the pore surface. In the case of MIL47(V), the interaction of acidic $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfS}$ is also reinforced through other basic centers of the porous matrix, such as the oxygens of the carboxylate groups and the $\pi$ electrons of the benzene rings. At higher pressures $\left(1.8\ \mathrm{MPa}\right)$ , the adsorbates arrange within the channel to form dimers and the strength of the hydrogen bonds becomes weaker. It should be noted that the strength of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\cdot$ –adsorbent interactions follow the sequence: MIL-53(Cr) $\mathbf{NP}>$ $\mathbf{ML}\mathbf{-}47(\mathbf{V})>\mathbf{ML}\mathbf{-}53(\mathbf{Cr})$ LP. This means that MIL-47(V) would be the most promising candidate for purification of gases containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ as a pollutant as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is more easily desorbed from MIL-47(V) than from MIL-53(Cr) NP. Preliminary simulations performed on MIL-47(V) suggest a high selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the range of pressures relevant for Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA). On the other hand, large-pore MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr) also show a high uptake of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ exhibiting type-I adsorption isotherms (16.7 and 38.4 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $2\ \mathrm{MPa}$ , respectively). However, the desorption appears to be irreversible, either because of a partial destruction of the framework or because of the strong interactions between the host and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ molecules. Their lack of ability to regenerate hampers the use of these mesoporous MOFs for the removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ from a practical application point of view.
M-CPO-27 also known as M-MOF-74 $\big[\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{2}\big(2,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{dhbdc}\big)(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\mathbf{O}})_{2}\big];$ (2,5-dhbdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{2,5}$ -dihydroxyterephthalate $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ) can bind $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ relatively strongly, allowing the storage of the gas for several months.6 It should be noted that in spite of the rather aggressive chemical nature of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , Ni-CPO-27 only shows slight loss in crystallinity after storage and release whereas Zn-CPO-27 was amorphized by prolonged storage over a year. The study of the interaction of Ni-CPO-27 with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ using powder X-ray diffraction and X-ray pair distribution function analysis reveals that $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ is clearly coordinated to coordinatively unsaturated metal sites. The release of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ from these MOFs has also been studied for biological applications taking into account the signal properties of this molecule. It has been demonstrated that a proportion of the stored hydrogen sulfide can be released by exposing these materials to moisture as the water molecules replace the adsorbate at the metal site. Thus, the contact of the $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfS}$ loaded materials with a moist atmosphere gives rise to the release of around one third of the chemisorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ from Ni-CPO-27 $\left(1.8\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right)$ and Zn-CPO-27 $(0.5~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ after $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The lower adsorptive and delivery potential of Zn-CPO-27 has been attributed to difficulties in the full activation of the sample7 and to the softer nature of $Z\mathrm{n}$ metal centres which gives rise to stronger and irreversible sulfur binding.
FMOF-2 $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{OH}(2,2^{\prime}$ -bis(4-carboxyphenyl)hexafluoropropane)1.5] is a breathing MOF, which has been tested for potential acid gas removal applications.8 Indeed, it remains structurally stable upon adsorption of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ reaching weight capacities of $14\%$ and $8.3\%$ , respectively, at 1 bar. Moreover, both gas adsorption– desorption isotherms show hysteresis confirming the breathing motion of FMOF-2 after gas adsorption–desorption.
Six isoreticular MOFs, namely MOF-5 $\mathrm{([Zn_{4}O(b d c)_{3}]},$ $\mathbf{bdc}={}$ 1,4-terephthalate), IRMOF-3 $([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{abdc})_{3}],$ , abdc $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{2}$ -amino1,4-benzene dicarboxylate), MOF-177 $([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{btb})_{3}],$ $\mathbf{btb}=1,3,5^{\circ}$ benzenetribenzoate), IRMOF-62 $\mathrm{([Zn_{4}O(b d b)_{3}]},$ , bdb $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ butadiynedibenzoate), Zn-CPO-27, and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ (bt $\mathtt{c}=1,3,5$ -tricarboxylate), prove to be very effective in removing gas and vapor contaminants, such as sulfur dioxide, ammonia, chlorine tetrahydrothiophene, benzene, dichloromethane and ethylene oxide (Fig. 1).9 The behaviour of each MOF has been compared to that of BPL carbon. In this section, only the behaviour related to the adsorption of toxic gases will be discussed (for the discussion of the capture of volatile organic compounds, please see below). Noteworthy, it has been demonstrated that the presence of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (i.e. Zn-CPO-27 and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}\right])$ and an amino functionality in IRMOF-3 prove to be effective in adsorbing ammonia and sulfur dioxide highly outperforming the behaviour of BPL carbon by a factor of at least 59 in the case of ammonia or performing equally well, in the case of sulfur dioxide (Fig. 1).
The improvement of ammonia adsorption in Zn-CPO-27 and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ is explained because of the presence of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ open metal sites, which behave as Lewis acids to coordinate ${\bf N H}_{3}$ Lewis bases, which is not possible in the activated carbon. Deeper studies on the interaction of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ have postulated that $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ reacts with ammonia to form a presumed diammine–copper(II) complex, under dry conditions.10 Under humid conditions, initially, water enhances ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption on $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ via dissolution of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in the water $\mathrm{{film}}^{11}$ but in a latter step $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{3}B T C}$ species are formed thereby inducing an irreversible loss of structure and porosity reflected in a significant decrease in available sites and capacity over multiple dynamic adsorption cycles of breakthrough curves.10 In the case of IRMOF-3, amine groups constitute reactive electron-rich groups available for hydrogen bonding with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules, improving the adsorption behavior in comparison with MOF-5 and BPL carbon. MOF-5, MOF-177 and IRMOF-62 lack any reactive functionality and show worse dynamic adsorption capacity for ammonia than the previously mentioned functionalized MOFs (Fig. 1). In spite of this, these MOFs show a better performance than BPL carbon towards this gas. However, they show little or no capacity for $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ .
Regarding chlorine adsorption, open metal sites in $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ are ineffective in this case as $\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ does not typically act as a ligand. However, halogen molecules can give rise to oxidative addition reactions to Pt redox active metal centers as demonstrated in the spin cross-over (SCO) $\{\mathrm{Fe(pyz)}[\mathrm{PtX}_{2n}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}]\}$ ${\big[}n=0$ to 1) materials as reported by Kitagawa, Real and colleagues.12 Moreover, the halogen addition reaction gives rise to a precise control of the SCO transition temperature of the resulting $\{\mathrm{Fe(pyz)}[\mathrm{PtX}_{2n}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}]\}$ materials. On the other hand, IRMOF-3 and IRMOF-62 have demonstrated considerable capacity for chlorine adsorption. Indeed, IRMOF-3 outperforms BPL carbon by a factor of 1.76 and the adsorption capacity of IRMOF-62 is in the same order of magnitude than the activated carbon. These results outline that despite the high capacities for thermodynamic gas adsorption, MOFs lacking reactive adsorption sites are ineffective in kinetic gas adsorption. In contrast, it should be noted that, in the case of MOFs with open metal sites (OMS) the presence of humidity may hamper the adsorption of the adsorbate of interest as a consequence of the irreversible coordination of water molecules to the open metal sites (see above). This feature is a main drawback for the application of MOFs containing OMS in competition with classical hydrophobic adsorbents like activated carbons (see also the section on the capture of volatile organic compounds).
Mavrandonakis and colleagues13 have recently carried out a computational study of the interaction of a series of toxic gases CO, OCS, $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ , NO, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}$ , ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ with the open metal sites of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ (Fig. 2). The calculated adsorption energies of the toxic gases and common adsorbate molecules follow the trend $\mathrm{NH_{3}}>\mathrm{H_{2}O}>\mathrm{PH_{3}}>\mathrm{H_{2}S}>\mathrm{SO_{2}}>\mathrm{CO}\sim\mathrm{OCS}\sim\mathrm{CO_{2}}\sim$ $\mathbf{N}_{y}\mathbf{O}_{x}>\mathbf{N}_{2}>\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Only ammonia is adsorbed more strongly on the metal sites than water, exceeding the water–framework interaction by $30\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . Noteworthy, although hydrogen sulfide and phosphane are less strongly bound by $10\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ compared to water, the interactions are significantly stronger than physisorption falling in the regime of chemisorption. These findings are in agreement with the experimental observations that ammonia, water and hydrogen sulfide decompose the framework.10
On the other hand, it has been demonstrated that it is possible to improve the performance of the adsorption capacities of toxic gases $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3},\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right.$ and $\mathbf{NO}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ ) using composites of MOFs (MOF-5, $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ and MIL-100(Fe)) with graphitic compounds (graphite or graphite oxide, GO) (Fig. 3).14 In the case of $\mathbf{MOF@GO}$ composites, the results are indicative of the strong coordination of the GO oxygen groups to the MOFs’ metallic centres, which in the case of MOF- $\mathrm{5\textcircled{\alGO}}$ and $[\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}]\mathbf{\textcircled{4}}\mathbf{Go}$ gives rise to the formation of a new pore space in the interface between the carbon layers and the MOF units. This new structural feature enhances the physical adsorption capacity of these composites towards toxic gases compared to the parent MOFs. For example, an increase of $12\%$ (for $\mathrm{NH}_{3.}^{\cdot}$ , $50\%$ (for $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ and $4\%$ (for $\mathbf{NO}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\Psi}}$ ) is observed in the case of $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]\textcircled{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{GO}$ in comparison with the original $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ . However, in addition to physisorption, reactive adsorption mechanisms of ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ also take place for these composite materials as well as for their parent MOFs (Fig. 3). In the case of the $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ based composites, the coordination of these toxic gases to the unsaturated Cu centers takes place and the formation of diammine–copper(II) complexes, CuS or $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ is proposed, depending on the adsorbate. These processes are accompanied by the decomposition of the framework with a significant loss of porosity. However, for the MIL100(Fe) and MOF-5 composites, which do not possess open metal sites, besides physisorption, acid–base reactions and hydrogen bonding take place, respectively, with the frameworks retaining their original porosity. The impact of the presence of water on the adsorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ was also studied for these composites. As observed for the parent MOFs, water enhances $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption in Cu-based composites and $\mathbf{MOF-}5\textcircled{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{GO}.$ . However, in the latter case, this fact is counterbalanced by the collapse of the MOF structure caused by the formation of hydrogen bonds between the water molecules and the zinc oxide tetrahedra. However, competitive adsorption of water and ${\bf N H}_{3}$ is observed on $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL}\mathbf{-}100\big(\mathbf{Fe}\big)@\mathbf{GO}.}\end{array}$ . The same competitive behavior is observed for water and $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ in $[\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}]\mathbf{\textcircled{4}}\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}$ .
The M-CPO-27 (M-MOF-74) $\mathbf{\Delta}\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+}\right.$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+})$ series have been tested for removing ${\bf N H}_{3}$ , CNCl and $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ from air in dry and humid conditions and dynamic breakthrough capacities were compared to 13X zeolite and BPL activated carbon.15 The results reveal that in the presence of a humid atmosphere at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ CPO-27 analogues adsorb an appreciable amount of water accounting at least for $0.1\mathrm{g}$ of water per gram of adsorbent. In dry conditions, the dynamic loadings of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ on all the CPO-27 analogues are generally near to or exceed the maximum loadings of traditional materials, such as 13X zeolite, which adsorbs $2.86~\mathrm{mol~kg}^{-1}$ under the same conditions, and BPL activated carbon, which has no capacity for dry ammonia.
The best performing material, Mg-CPO-27 loads three times the capacity of 13X zeolite and the worst performing analog, Ni-CPO-27 loads $80\%$ of the capacity of 13X. It should be noted that when ammonia is loaded in dry conditions, a significant portion of the adsorbate is retained when the MOF materials are exposed to dry air during desorption. However, the 13X zeolite did not retain any of the adsorbed ammonia under the same breakthrough conditions. As expected, the adsorptive capacity of these materials is reduced in a humid gas stream, but they maintain significant adsorption capacity for ${\bf N H}_{3}$ . Indeed, Mg-CPO-27, the worst performing material under humid conditions, shows 6 times the capacity of BPL activated carbon. On the other hand, CPO-27 structures adsorb dry cyanogen chloride in the same order of magnitude as classical materials, reaching $70\%$ of the retention by Co-CPO-27 even after desorption. However, under humid conditions, none of these MOFs adsorb an appreciable quantity of CNCl resembling the behaviour of 13X zeolite in both dry and humid conditions. Regarding $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ removal, only Mg-CPO-27 loads this acid gas. In general, Co and Mg analogues show better adsorption of the four gases in both dry and humid conditions. Furthermore, it is clear that the presence of water diminished the adsorption capacity of these MOFs towards the studied gases as a consequence of the competition of the water molecules with the toxic gases for the adsorption at the metal centres leading to diminished breakthrough times and total capacities.
The presence of open metal sites is also highly beneficial for the capture of NO. A series of MOFs have been assayed for the capture and release of NO for biological applications. This is the case for $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ and CPO-27 structures,16 which adsorb up to 3 and 8 mmol of NO per gram of MOF, respectively, considerably more than other similar nanoporous solids, such as zeolites. However, the adsorption of NO on $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ can be considered nearly irreversible as only $1\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ of NO per gram of material is released upon exposure to moisture while CPO-27 materials completely desorb all the stored NO under similar conditions. Other biodegradable materials, such as the Fe-based MIL-88A $\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{CO}_{2}^{-})\right]$ (fumarate)3], MIL-88B $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{OX}(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc})_{3}],$ $1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}=1,4$ 4-benzenedicarboxylate ( $\mathbf{\tilde{X}}=\mathbf{F} $ Cl, OH), $\mathbf{MIL}–88\mathbf{B}–\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ $\big[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{OX}\big(2{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}\big)_{3}\big],$ , ${\sf2{\mathrm{-}}N O_{2}{\mathrm{-}}b d c}=2$ -nitroterephthalate $\mathbf{\left(X=F\right.}$ , Cl, OH) and MIL-88B-2OH $\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{OX}(2,5\mathrm{-dhbdc})_{3}\right]$ , 2,5-dhbdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalate ( $\mathbf{\tilde{X}}=\mathbf{F} $ , Cl, OH), show loading capacities lying within the $1{-}2.5~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ range.17 In this case, the low accessibility to the pores is due to the absence of a breathing mechanism of the structure with the pores remaining closed and preventing the easy diffusion of NO to bind the accessible iron metal sites. The released amount of NO from these structures in the presence of a wet gas is even lower, a few tenths of mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is attributed to the desorption of the physisorbed NO prior to the desorption tests, but it is still enough to ensure a significant release at the biological level over prolonged periods of time. A similar behaviour is observed in BioMIL-3 $[\mathrm{Ca}_{2}(\mathrm{azbz-TC})\cdot$ $\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\big)(\mathrm{DMF})\big].$ , azbz- $\mathbf{\cdotTC}=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -azobenzenetetracarboxylate, which adsorbs $0.8\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of NO at 1 atm, a quantity which is much lower than the theoretical unsaturated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ metal sites.18 The absence of a plateau in the NO adsorption branch suggests a possible higher adsorption at higher pressures. However, residual coordinated DMF, steric hindrance and diffusion constraints make coordination on the unsaturated metal sites more difficult. These factors also contribute to the slow and low delivery of NO from this material $(5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ overnight). Other methodologies have been developed to store NO in MOFs. This is the case for the postsynthetic functionalization of the open metal sites in $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ with the bifunctional 4-(methylamino)-pyridine to produce secondary amines, which are able to react with NO to yield the diazen-1-ium-1,2-diolate (NONOate) derivative.19 A similar strategy consists in the functionalization of the organic linkers with amines available to interact with NO yielding NONOate compounds.
CO adsorption has also been studied in MOFs with openmetal sites. It has been demonstrated that $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ preferentially adsorbs CO towards $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . This can be explained because of the electrostatic interactions between the partial charge of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites and the CO dipole.20 On the other hand, Ni-CPO-27 strongly coordinates CO at room temperature.21 Most of the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites are involved in the interaction (about $80\%$ ), forming 1 : 1 linear adducts whose interaction enthalpy is slightly above $50\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . However, the adsorption is completely reversible at room temperature after prolonged evacuation. As well as for NO, the biocompatible series MIL-88B and $\mathbf{MIL}–88\mathbf{B}–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ $\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{X}(2\mathbf{-N}\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{-b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{c})_{3}\right]$ , $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - bdc $=2$ -aminoterephthalate ( $\mathbf{\check{X}}=\mathbf{F} $ , Cl, OH), have been studied for the capture and release of CO for therapeutic applications showing that CO binding occurs to unsaturated $\mathrm{Fe^{H}/F e^{I I I}}$ coordination sites generated by the activation procedure.22
Finally, noteworthy, the cages of ZIF-8 $\left[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{m})_{2}\right]$ $\left(2{\cdot}\mathbf{MeImH}\ =\ \ 2{\cdot}\mathbf{\Lambda}\right.$ -methylimidazole) bind iodine 4 times more strongly than activated carbon, the traditional high capacity iodine adsorbent.23 The latter result is of relevance in order to capture radioactive iodine isotopes produced in nuclear processes.
## Capture of volatile organic compounds
The characteristics of adsorbent materials for the capture of volatile organic compounds can be significantly different from the ones for gases. In this regard, the diffusion kinetics of vapor molecules might be very slow in narrow pore materials and, on the other hand, the presence of open metal sites can be sometimes disadvantageous for air/gas purification purposes in the presence of moisture.
As mentioned in the previous section, six isoreticular MOFs, namely MOF-5, IRMOF-3, MOF-177, IRMOF-62, Zn-CPO-27, and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ are shown as selective adsorbents of tetrahydrothiophene, benzene, dichloromethane and ethylene oxide.9 In the same line as the results of breakthrough experiments on gaseous contaminants, MOF-5 and MOF-177 do not perform well as kinetic adsorption media. IRMOF-62 is largely outclassed by BPL carbon except in the case of ethylene oxide adsorption, for which both materials are equally ineffective. IRMOF-3 is a poor adsorbent for the vapors chosen, because none of them behave as good Lewis acids. MOFs containing open metal sites are found to be the most effective in removing vapors from the gas stream. Zn-CPO-27 and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ outperform BPL carbon by an order of magnitude in ethylene oxide adsorption. However, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Zn-CPO-}27$ is not effective against the entire range of vapors, whereas $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ is effective. $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ outperforms BPL carbon in tetrahydrothiophene adsorption by a factor of 3 although there is essentially no difference in performance between the activated carbon and $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ in dichloromethane and benzene adsorption. It should be noted, however, that the presence of moisture can significantly diminish the performance of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ as a consequence of the blockage of the open metal sites by water molecules (see below).
MIL-101 $\left([\mathbf{Cr}_{3}\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{c})]\right)$ has proved to be effective in the adsorption of a wide range of VOCs with various functional groups and polarities, i.e. acetone, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, $n$ -hexane, methanol, butanone, dichloromethane and $n$ -butylamine. Yan et al. demonstrated that MIL-101 has higher affinity for VOCs containing heteroatoms or aromatic rings, especially the amines.24 In addition, MIL-101 shows higher adsorption capacities for acetone, toluene, ethylbenzene and xylenes than other commonly used adsorbents, such as zeolites, resins, activated carbon together with their derivatives.25 For example, MIL-101 adsorbs 16.7 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ of benzene at $288\mathrm{K}$ and 56 mbar, which is almost twice the values for zeolites (silicalite-1 and SBA-15) and 3–5 times larger than that of activated carbons (Ajax and ACF). In this case, the profile of the thermally programmed desorption curve shows two separated peaks corresponding to two major sites for benzene adsorption. The strong interactions would occur between the $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ metal centers and the adsorbate molecules and the slightly weaker interactions take place between the space of the pores and the adsorbate. Consecutive cycles of adsorption–desorption show that fast desorption kinetics, high desorption efficiency and stable adsorption capacity over five cycles are available. The efficiency of benzene desorption can reach $97\%$ exhibiting a high reversibility of benzene adsorption–desorption.26
On the other hand, the modulation of the porous network of the anionic MOF $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}[C u_{3}(O H)(c a p z)](c a p z=4\cdot}$ -carboxypyrazolato) by means of ion-exchange processes of the extra-framework $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ cations leads to profound changes in the textural properties of its porous surface and in the adsorption selectivity towards benzene– cyclohexane mixtures.27 Indeed, exposure of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{A}}[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{capz})]$ materials $\mathrm{(A=NH_{4}}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{\bfMe}}_{3}\mathrm{{NH}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{NH}^{+\gamma}$ to benzene– cyclohexane $1{:}1$ vapor mixtures shows significant enrichment of the adsorbate phase in the benzene component. This behaviour is explained taking into account that although the increasing bulk of the exchanged cations still permits access of molecules to the porous structure, a concomitant increase in size-exclusion selectivity takes place.
The flexible MIL-53(Al) framework adsorbs xylene isomers and ethylbenzene.28 Adsorption isotherms at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ show two well-defined steps and hysteresis, corresponding to the opening or breathing of the framework, as induced by the presence of adsorbing molecules. At low pressures, adsorption leads to framework contraction. In the limited space of the closed form of the MIL-53(Al) framework, only a single-file arrangement of molecules can be accommodated, adsorbed along the length of the pores. At much higher pressures, the pores are reopened.
In the open form, there is space enough for the xylene isomers to be adsorbed in pairs along the length of the pores, leading to a doubling of the amount adsorbed (Fig. 4). Due to differences in the efficiencies with which the different isomers can be packed inside the pores, the adsorption capacity is highly affected. This phenomenon is known as commensurate adsorption and occurs when the molecular size and shape of the adsorbate lead to an orientation and adsorbed amount that is compatible and self-consistent with the crystal symmetry and pore structure of the adsorbent.29
A modified MOF, $\mathrm{{CuCl}}_{2}$ -loaded MIL-47(V) shows remarkable adsorption capacity of benzothiophene.30 MIL-47(V) shows an unexpected reduction ability to form ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ ions from loaded $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions probably because of the presence of $\mathbf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ in the MOF. The obtained ${\mathrm{cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ ions show a beneficial effect on the adsorption of benzothiophene probably through $\pi$ -complexation. It has been demonstrated that the adsorption capacity increases with increasing $\mathrm{\cu{Cl}}_{2}$ loading up to a specific content $(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{V}=0.05\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and decreases with further increase in the $\mathrm{\cu{Cl}_{2}}$ content, showing that there is an optimum $\mathrm{{CuCl}}_{2}$ concentration. This is due to the contribution of both the porosity/acidity of MIL-47(V) and a ${\mathrm{cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ site derived from $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ .
On the other hand, the development of sensor materials is also a very active field of research. In this regard, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}.$ $\left({\mathrm{dpNDI}}\right)]_{n}$ $\mathrm{\boldmath~\cal~H_{2}b d c~}=$ benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid; $\mathbf{dpNDI}\ =$ $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenediimide) gives rise to strong stacking interactions with VOC molecules with a concomitant strong change in the emission color sensitive to the ionization potential of each molecule (Fig. 5).31
In this context, the $\{\mathrm{Fe(pyz)[Pt(CN)_{4}]}\}$ system can incorporate a variety of vapor guest molecules, namely benzene and $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ , which modify the pore size and optical and magnetic properties of the materials and might be useful for sensing purposes.32
As mentioned before, MOFs with coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, such as $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ , are ineffective for the capture of VOCs in the presence of ambient moisture. With the aim of overcoming this problem, we have reported a novel flexible MOF, namely $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{bpb})]^{33}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{bpb=}1,4$ -(4-bispyrazolyl)benzene), which is efficient for the adsorption of benzene and cyclohexane. In addition, this material captures tetrahydrothiophene (up to $\boldsymbol{0.34\ g}$ of tetrahydrothiophene per gram of material) from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}{\mathrm{-CO}}_{2}$ mixtures in dynamic conditions even in the presence of humidity $(60\%)$ , thus overcoming the problems raised by prototypic MOF-5 and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ MOF materials in practical applications.
As previously mentioned in the introduction, a special family of VOCs is the warfare agents. The search for efficient adsorbents of these harmful molecules in practical applications is of great interest. It should be noted that in real conditions, humidity will always be present and water molecules will constitute an important competitor in the adsorption process warfare agents is an adequate strategy for the selective adsorption of these molecules even in extreme humid conditions. The basis of this strategy remains in the high affinity of the porous matrix towards the non-polar warfare agents in contrast with its low affinity for polar water molecules. For example, the MOF-5 analog $\mathrm{[Zn_{4}O(d m c a p z)_{3}]}$ (dmcapz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{3,5}$ -dimethyl-4-carboxypyrazolato) is suitable for the capture of diisopropylfluorophosphate (DIFP, model of sarin nerve gas) and diethylsulfide (DES, model of vesicant mustard gas).34 This compound shows remarkable chemical, mechanical and thermal stability granted by the nature of the M–N,O(carboxypyrazolato) coordinative bonds. In addition, it is highly hydrophobic, which results in large ${\tt V O C}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ partition coefficients. Under ambient conditions, $[\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(d m c a p z)_{3}}]$ clearly outperforms the behavior of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ which, when hydrated, does not retain either DES or DIFP. However, the performance of $\mathrm{[Zn_{4}O(d m c a p z)_{3}]}$ approaches that of molecular sieve activated carbon Carboxen suggesting similar adsorption processes dominated by the small size and apolar nature of the pores in both materials. Then, it has been clearly demonstrated the necessity of stabilizing MOFs against ambient humidity to turn these porous materials more suitable for specialized and industrial applications. In this context, preor postsynthetic modification approaches have been explored in order to enhance the hydrophobicity of MOFs. One of the most successful approaches is the introduction of alkane or fluoroalkane35 residues in the organic linkers. In this context, Omary and colleagues36 reported the synthesis of totally fluorinated MOFs, coined FMOF-1 and FMOF-2, from the combination of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ with 3,5-bis(trifluoromethyl)-1,2,4-triazolate. The hydrophobic character of FMOF-1 is mainly responsible for the observed efficient selective adsorption ability for aliphatic and aromatic oil components (benzene, toluene, $p$ -xylene, cyclohexane and $n$ -hexane), while preventing the entrance of water molecules into their pores. Likewise, the isoreticular series $\mathrm{[Ni_{8}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{6}]}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathbf{L}1=1H\mathbf{\tilde{\Gamma}}$ pyrazole-4-carboxylic acid; $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}2=$ 4-( $1H\cdot$ -pyrazol-4-yl)benzoic acid, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}3=4,4^{\prime}$ -benzene-1,4-diylbis( $\mathrm{i}H\cdot$ -pyrazole), $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}4=4\mathbf{,}4^{\prime}$ -buta-1,3-diyne-1,4-diylbis( $1H\cdot$ -pyrazole), $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}5=4,4^{\prime}$ -(benzene-1,4-diyldiethyne-2,1-diyl)bis( $1H\cdot$ -pyrazole), $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}5\mathbf{-R}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{R}_{\alpha}}=\mathbf{\tilde{\Gamma}}$ methyl, trifluoromethyl)) proves that the use of metal–azolate coordinative bonds gives rise to materials with enhanced stability towards hydrolysis.35 Moreover, the length and functionalization of the linkers impact on the pore size as well as on the surface polarity (Fig. 6). The most significant results regarding the capture of warfare agents were achieved under a strongly competitive moist atmosphere (up to $80\%$ RH) and have been compared with the hydrophobic active carbon Blu¨cher-101408, which is the active phase of Saratoga filtering systems. Indeed, ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR analysis of the adsorbate phase after the adsorption process of DES in $80\%$ RH moist streams reveals that only $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{L}5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{6}\right]$ and Blu¨cher-101408 efficiently capture DES under these conditions, witnessing that the adsorption of DES in this MOF is not affected by the presence of humidity. This isoreticular series might be considered as the first step towards the rational design of MOFs for the capture of harmful VOCs under highly demanding environmental conditions.
## 2. Catalytic degradation of harmful gases and volatile organic compounds
The catalytic degradation of harmful gases and vapors into nontoxic substances should be regarded as the ideal case for gas/air purification processes since it overcomes the problems related to adsorbent saturation or its behavior as secondary emitters. Noteworthy, the development of tailored heterogeneous catalysts is one of the areas of major current interest in MOFs research, since these materials are amenable for finely tuning their catalytic properties.37 However, there are still few reports in which the catalytic activity of MOF materials has been applied for the removal of harmful substances from gas/air streams.
## Catalytic decomposition of trace gases and vapors
CO oxidation to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be achieved by gold nanoparticles (NPs) deposited on zeolite-type metal–organic framework ZIF-8 $\left[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{m})_{2}\right]$ by a simple solid grinding method. The catalytic activity of the resulting $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}{-}8$ material for CO oxidation is improved along with increasing Au loadings with the highest catalytic activity being obtained for $5.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Au@ZIF-8, which presents half conversion of CO at $\sim170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Gold NPs are close to being monodispersed and do not give rise to aggregation during catalytic reactions over multiple cycles.38 This reaction might be useful for hydrogen purification purposes for fuel cell applications. The 3D $\mathrm{\Delta[Yb(OH)(2,6\mathrm{-}A Q D S)(H_{2}O)]}$ (AQDS: anthraquinone-2,6-disulfonate)39 polymeric framework acts as a good catalyst in hydrodesulfurization reactions of thiophene under mild conditions (7 bar of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ ) as a consequence of the low coordination of the Yb atom and/or lability of coordinated ligands. The enhancement of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ trapping has also been achieved by impregnating $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ with $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ salts $\left(\mathbf{BaCl}_{2}\right)$ , $\mathrm{Ba}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Ba}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}]$ . Indeed, the $\mathbf{BaCl}_{2}\mathbf{-}[\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}]$ composite is able to capture $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ in an oxidative atmosphere in the form of sulfates outperforming the reference material $\mathbf{BaCO}_{3}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}/\mathbf{Pt}.$ ,40 which is the first generation storage reduction catalyst known to store $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ . The formation of small microcrystals of $\mathbf{Ba(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Ba}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}$ in the pores of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ is observed, while the MOF structure is maintained. However, after impregnation with $\mathbf{BaCl}_{2}$ , the parent MOF loses crystallinity and the $\mathbf{BaCl}_{2}$ appears highly dispersed in the material. The $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ uptake of all Ba– $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ samples exceeded the stoichiometric uptake capacity based on the $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{a}^{2+}$ concentration, which is indicative that a fraction of $\mathbf{SO}_{x}$ is bound to $\mathrm{cu}$ cations. At low temperatures, when the MOF still maintains its structural integrity, the $\mathrm{so}_{x}$ storage is due to the concomitant adsorption of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ on ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and the formation of Ba-sulfates. However, at high temperatures $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ decomposes leading to isolated Cu species that react with $\mathbf{SO}_{x}$ to form Cu-sulfates. This process is of interest in the elimination of traces of sulfur compounds in fuels prior to the $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ storage-reduction $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{x}–\mathrm{SR}\right)$ concept. However, irreversible $\mathbf{SO}_{x}$ adsorption is the main drawback of the above described materials.
$\left[\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{OF(bdc)}_{2}\right]$ MIL-101 $(\mathbf{M}\ =\mathbf{\Gamma}\mathbf{Cr} $ , Fe) have also been recently found to be active for solvent-free selective oxidation of cyclohexane with molecular oxygen and/or tert-butyl hydroperoxide under mild conditions.41 Similarly, the zeomimetic $\mathrm{NH_{4}[C u_{3}(O H)(c a p z)]}$ system is also able to catalyse the oxidation of cyclohexane and cyclohexene with tert-butyl hydroperoxide under mild conditions.27
Polyoxometalates (POMs) are regarded as active acid and oxidation catalysts in the homogeneous phase. Noteworthy, POMs can be incorporated in the structure of MOFs either as building blocks or encapsulated in the pore structure of MOFs thereby enhancing their stability and giving rise to advanced heterogeneous catalysts.42 In this regard, Hill and co-workers have shown that the redox active POM $\mathrm{[CuPW_{11}O_{39}]^{5-}}$ encapsulated in $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ might be useful for the catalytic air based oxidative degradation of $\mathrm{SH}_{2}$ and mercaptane molecules (Fig. 7).43 Indeed, the $\left[\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}\right]@[\mathbf{CuPW}_{11}\mathbf{O}_{39}]$ hybrid material catalyzes the rapid chemo- and shape-selective oxidation of thiols into disulfides and, more significantly, the rapid and sustained removal of toxic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ via $\mathrm{H}_{2}S+1/2\mathrm{O}_{2}\to1/8\mathrm{S}_{8}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (4000 turnovers in $<20\mathrm{~h~}$ ) while the POM or the MOF alone is catalytically slow or inactive. Similarly, the redox active POM $\mathrm{[CoPW_{11}O_{39}]^{5-}}$ incorporated inside $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathbf{OF(bdc)}_{2}\right]$ MIL-101(Cr) voids behaves as an active heterogeneous catalyst for the molecular oxygen allylic oxidation of $\mathfrak{a}$ -pinene, which can be used as a flavoring substance and drug precursor.44
Lin and co-workers45 have recently shown that it is possible to obtain oxidation and photoactive catalysts by doping of $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b p d c)_{6}]}$ (UiO-67), (bpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ para-biphenyldicarboxylic acid), with $\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ metal fragments attached to $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine$^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylato spacers (Fig. 8). The resulting modified MOF-6 containing the phosphorescent $\mathrm{\small{[Ru^{H}(b p y)_{2}(d c b p y)]C l_{2}(H_{2}L_{6})(b p y=}}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) behaves as an efficient aerobic oxidation catalyst of thioanisole (Fig. 8).
The use of MOF materials with intrinsic photochemical properties is also an advantageous feature for the possible degradation of harmful substances. In this regard, $\mathrm{[Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(b d c)_{6}]}$ $\left(\mathbf{MIL-1}25\right)^{46}$ under UV-irradiation is able to oxidize adsorbed alcohols giving rise to a photochromic behaviour associated with the formation of stable mixed valence titanium-oxo compounds (Fig. 9). The titanium oxo clusters are back oxidized in the presence of oxygen closing the catalytic cycle. The photogenerated electrons are trapped as $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers, while a concomitant oxidation of the adsorbed alcohol molecules occurs. Noteworthy, the photocatalytic properties of $\mathrm{[Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(b d c)_{6}]}$ (MIL-125) can be tuned by replacing bdc by 2-aminoterephthalate.
The ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf M I L-125$ system behaves as an active photocatalyst under visible light irradiation in the reduction of carbon dioxide to the formate anion with TEOA (triethanolamine) acting as an electron donor and a base.47
## Catalytic degradation of chemical warfare agents
The catalytic degradation of the highly toxic chemical warfare agents is of high interest, since it avoids the problems related to the manipulation of the contaminated materials after exposure to such substances. In this regard, the usual degradation pathways of such substances involve oxidation as well as hydrolysis routes (Scheme 1). This fact has led to the use of redox and/or acid–base active MOFs as catalysts for the remediation systems after exposure to these extremely harmful molecules.
In this regard, the presence of Cu open metal sites with Lewis acidic nature in $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ favors the hydrolysis, although slow, of both sulfur mustard and organophosphate nerve agents.48
The catalytic activity in hydrolysis reactions of the $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ type systems can be improved by the incorporation of acid polyoxometalates in their pore structure. Thus, Su, Liu and colleagues have taken advantage of the improved hydrolytic activity of the sodalite type $\mathrm{H_{3}[(C u_{4}C l)_{3}(b t c)_{8}]_{2}[P W_{12}O_{40}]_{3}(C_{4}H_{12}N)_{6}}$ (NENU-11) system for the capture and posterior hydrolytic degradation of the model nerve agent dimethylmethylphosphonate (DMMP).49
Hatton and colleagues50 have reported the incorporation of highly nucleophilic 4-methylaminopyridine residues by means of covalent coupling of the amino residues of aluminium aminoterephthalate MOF systems to give rise to $\mathrm{\small{[Al_{3}O F(4-M A P-N H\cdot b d c)_{2}]}}$ $\left(\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathbf{Al})\right)$ and [AlOH(4-MAP-NH-bdc)] (4-MAP-NH-MIL53(Al)) active materials (Fig. 10). Noteworthy, this MOF can be incorporated onto protective rubber glove surfaces prior to curing giving rise to self-detoxifying surfaces exhibiting a high catalytic activity in the hydrolytic degradation of chemical warfare simulant diisopropylfluorophosphate (DIFP) as proven by $^{31}\mathbf{P}.$ -MAS NMR studies.
## Conclusions
The use of MOFs with adequate pore size/shape is not enough for an efficient capture of hazardous gases/vapors and other more specific interactions between the harmful adsorbates and the host are desirable. For example, the presence of open metal sites (coordinatively unsaturated metal centres) or certain functionalizations on the pore surface may enhance the adsorption selectivity/efficiency of MOFs towards certain toxic compounds via coordination bonds, acid–base/electrostatic interactions, $\pi$ -complex/H-bonding formation, etc. Thus, an effective strategy for the synthesis of materials that strongly retain one gas in preference to another is to target a particular surface chemistry instead of optimizing the surface area alone. Adsorption selectivity can, however, become a drawback for protective applications (i.e. fire rescuers or army applications). In this regard, the fabrication of multicomponent composite materials (i.e. core shell adsorbents, hybrid textiles and thin film coatings of MOFs on various substrates) is becoming a common practice, which provides a way to enhance the performance of the materials towards the generation of multi-target adsorbents. Moreover, the design of chemically, thermally and mechanically robust MOF materials able to stand the typical working conditions of practical applications (i.e. moisture, strong oxidising/reducing agents, acidic/basic environments, high temperature/pressure. . .) needs to be addressed by means of the right choice of metal– ligand combination, synthetic and postsynthetic conditions, formation of composite materials, etc.
Noteworthy, the amenable properties of MOFs offers a way towards the development of advanced catalysts suitable for the degradation of harmful gases and vapors into non-toxic substances. This strategy should be regarded as the ideal case for gas/air purification processes since it overcomes the problems related to adsorbent saturation or its behavior as secondary emitters. Finally, the integration of these catalysts into composite materials may lead to advanced filters, textiles and surfaces with self-cleaning properties.
